[38;2;124;124;124mA non-adaptive strategy: B fixes S_1, ..., S_m in advance. Each x has a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msignature σ(x) = (b_1, ..., b_m) ∈ {0,1}^m. The answer string is a = (a_1, ..., [0m[38;2;124;124;124ma_m). The set of consistent x values is {x : σ(x) and a differ only in positions[0m[38;2;124;124;124mwith no k+1 consecutive}.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mB wants this set to have size ≤ 2^k for every answer string a.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe number of binary strings σ that are "compatible" with a given a (differing [0m
[38;2;124;124;124monly in positions with no k+1 consecutive) is the number of subsets of {1,...,m}[0m[38;2;124;124;124mwith no k+1 consecutive elements. Let's call this F(m, k+1).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mF(m, k+1) = number of subsets of {1,...,m} with no (k+1) consecutive elements.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor k+1 = 2 (k=1): F(m, 2) = Fibonacci number F_{m+2}.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mFor general k+1: F(m, k+1) satisfies the recurrence F(m, k+1) = F(m-1, k+1) + [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mF(m-2, k+1) + ... + F(m-k-1, k+1) (include position m or not, if not, can [0m
[38;2;124;124;124minclude m-1, etc., but with no k+1 consecutive).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the number of subsets of {1,...,m} with no k+1 consecutive elements [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msatisfies:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mF(m) = F(m-1) + F(m-2) + ... + F(m-k-1) for m ≥ k+1, with F(0) = 1, F(1) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2, ..., F(k) = 2^k.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a (k+1)-generalized Fibonacci sequence. The growth rate is ρ_{k+1}^m [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwhere ρ_{k+1} is the largest root of x^{k+1} = x^k + x^{k-1} + ... + 1, i.e., [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mx^{k+1} = (x^{k+1} - 1)/(x-1), so x^{k+2} - 2x^{k+1} + 1 = 0.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor large k, ρ_{k+1} → 2 (since 2^{k+2} - 2·2^{k+1} + 1 = 1 > 0, and (2-ε)^{k+2}[0m[38;2;124;124;124m- 2(2-ε)^{k+1} + 1 ≈ -ε(k+2)2^{k+1} + 1 < 0 for large k).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mMore precisely, ρ_{k+1} = 2 - Θ(2^{-k}). So ρ_{k+1}^m ≈ 2^m · (1 - Θ(2^{-k}))^m [0m[38;2;124;124;124m≈ 2^m · e^{-m·Θ(2^{-k})}.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor B to have ≤ 2^k compatible candidates, B needs N · (compatible signatures [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mper answer string) / 2^m ≤ 2^k. Wait, this isn't right because B assigns [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msignatures to candidates.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf B uses unique signatures (each candidate gets a distinct m-bit string), then [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe number of compatible candidates is at most F(m, k+1). B needs F(m, k+1) ≤ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2^k. But F(m, k+1) grows like ρ_{k+1}^m, which is > 1 for m ≥ 1. So F(m, k+1) ≤ [0m[38;2;124;124;124m2^k requires m to be small, but then B can't distinguish N candidates.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf B uses non-unique signatures (multiple candidates per signature), then the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnumber of compatible candidates is at most (N / 2^m) · F(m, k+1) (if signatures [0m[38;2;124;124;124mare evenly distributed). B needs (N / 2^m) · F(m, k+1) ≤ 2^k.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo N · F(m, k+1) / 2^m ≤ 2^k, i.e., F(m, k+1) / 2^m ≤ 2^k / N.[0m

